WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Good morning John. A couple warnings about this video: first, if you find cuteness offensive,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.920
you should probably leave now. Also, if you are prone to squee, take the necessary precaution.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.120
Today's video is about the tiniest things.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.000
I'm doing this, of course, because the world's smallest chameleon was recently discovered

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.180
in Madagascar, and it is very tiny. And I'm also doing it because the episode of SciShow

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.260
that we did on tardigrades went really really well. Honey badger don't care? Really?

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.460
Maybe shove honey badger out of an airlock into the vacuum of space and see if he cares then.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.640
Tardigrade don't care. Dun dun dun dun TARDIGRADES!

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.100
This little guy, while being very, very, tiny, is not the smallest reptile.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:39.100
That honour goes to... the Virgin Island dwarf gecko,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.780
pictured here, full grown, on a dime.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.640
The  Virgin Island dwarf gecko does not, however, take the prize for smallest vertebrate,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.260
that either goes to a frog or a fish, depending on which side of the controversy you're on.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.600
It makes sense that the smallest vertebrate would be water dwelling,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.260
because they do not have to deal with the pressure of gravity as much because they get to be under water.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.240
Now, I would tell you the name of this frog, but I can't pronounce it.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.380
Here it is pictured on a dime, because apparently that's the thing to do.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:08.460
Unless you're the world's smallest snake, in which case you're so massive that you have to be pictured on a quarter.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.540
For people who aren't American, here is a dime, it's our smallest coin,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.660
even though it's worth more than pennies and nickels,

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.080
and this is a quarter. So that's how big those animals are.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.680
But even smaller than that frog, is a fish.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.720
A fish that we Nerdfighters know a little bit about,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.800
but I did not know that it was the smallest vertebrate. THE MALE ANGLERFISH!

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.640
Though, most biologists don't count it, because it relies on sexual parasitism to survive.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.000
But WHATEVER! I'm on the anglerfish side of that debate.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.020
I'm on the anglerfish side of every debate!

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.180
Okay, I'm willing to admit that so far, this hasn't been particularly cute.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.540
But we're going to change that around by now going to smallest warm-blooded animals.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:48.300
And the smallest warm-blooded creature is the bee hummingbird

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.600
and oh, it's a little cutie!

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.320
The smallest mammal, however, is harder to determine.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.180
By length, the smallest mammal is Kitti's hog-nosed bat,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.720
which I think is adorable,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.040
and by mass, the Etruscan shrew is the smallest mammal.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.680
So I'm gonna go with the Etruscan shrew, because mass seems like the one you wanna go with there,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.280
and oh my god, is it cute?

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.540
There was, however, once upon a time, a smaller mammal:

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.040
the long extinct Batodonoides vanhouteni, a kind of shrew,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.900
which I only mention because of the Van Houten.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.020
And, finally, our last wild animal, the world's smallest primate,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.720
Madame Berthe's mouse lemur.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:29.740
It weighs just over an ounce, and it is sho shquishy,  [unintelligible 'this is so cute I can't talk properly' noises]

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.920
Shut up! And then we have a selection of domestic animals.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.260
Ducky, 1.4 pounds, the current smallest dog.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.680
Thumbelina, a miniature horse that turned out to be way more miniature than any other miniature horse

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.120
at just 17 inches tall, which does not sound healthy.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.660
And we're finishing off on Mr. Peebles. Hello Mr. Peebles.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:52.740
Mr. Peebles is my favorite animal of this video, because it led me to the google search of 'cat in a cup',

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.460
where I found SO MANY CATS IN CUPS!

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.880
[singing with music in the background] It's a cat in a cup,

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.880
it's a cat in a cup, girl...

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.640
It's a cat in a cup!

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.560
Oh yeah. It's a cat in a cuuuuuupppp.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:11.620
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

